{"cuisine/greenlandic-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/greenlandic-cuisine","n":"Greenlandic cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Greenland","intro":[["Greenlandic cuisine is traditionally based on meat from marine mammals, birds, and fish, and normally contains high levels of protein. Since colonization and the arrival of international trade, the cuisine has been increasingly influenced by Danish, British, American and Canadian cuisine. During the summer when the weather is milder, meals are often eaten outdoors."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"greenlandic-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenlandic_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Takanna: Delicious Food from Greenland","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Tupaarnaq Rosing Olsen","note":"Greenlandic recipes from a Greenlandic author, illustrated by Nina Spore Kreutzmann and published by Milik in Greenland.","url":"https://milik.gl/en/book/takanna-delicious-food-from-greenland","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/27/Phoeca_groenlandica_piece_of_meat_upernavik_2007-06-26.JPG/500px-Phoeca_groenlandica_piece_of_meat_upernavik_2007-06-26.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Kim Hansen","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phoeca_groenlandica_piece_of_meat_upernavik_2007-06-26.JPG"}},"cuisine/indonesian-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/indonesian-chinese-cuisine","n":"Indonesian Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Chinese Indonesian cuisine (Indonesian: Masakan Tionghoa-Indonesia, simplified Chinese: 印尼中华料理; traditional Chinese: 印尼中華料理; pinyin: yìnní zhōnghuá liàolǐ or (Ìn-nî) Tn̂g-lâng-chia̍k (印尼唐人食) is characterized by the mixture of Chinese with local Indonesian style. Chinese Indonesians, mostly descendant of Han, Hokkien and Hakka people, brought their legacy of Chinese cuisine, and modified some of the dishes with the addition of Indonesian ingredients, such as kecap manis (sweet soy sauce), palm sugar, peanut sauce, chili, santan (coconut milk) and local spices to form a hybrid Chinese-Indonesian cuisine. Some of the dishes and cakes share the same style as in Malaysia and Singapore, known as Nyonya cuisine by the Peranakan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"indonesian-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesian_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/96/Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg/500px-Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg","w":500,"h":394,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg"}},"cuisine/corsican-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/corsican-cuisine","n":"Corsican cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in France","intro":[["The cuisine of Corsica is the traditional cuisine of the island of Corsica. It is mainly based on the products of the island, and due to historical and geographical reasons, has much in common with Italian cuisine, and marginally with those of Nice and Provence."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"corsican-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corsican_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Campanile (cake)","dish/campanile-cake"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Corsica: The Recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nicolas Stromboni","note":"Traditional Corsican dishes collected by Nicolas Stromboni, published by Smith Street Books.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781925418521","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781925418521"},{"name":"Recipes from Corsica","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Christiane Schapira","note":"English translation of a collection of Corsican home recipes, from Prospect Books.","url":"https://prospectbooks.co.uk/products-page/current-titles/recipes-from-corsica","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/85/Pulenda-1.jpg/500px-Pulenda-1.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Pierre Bona","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulenda-1.jpg"}},"ingredient/vinegar":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/vinegar","n":"Vinegar","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 18 dishes across 23 cuisines","intro":[["Vinegar (from Old French  vyn egre 'sour wine') is an odorous aqueous solution of diluted acetic acid and trace compounds that may include flavorings or naturally occurring organic compounds. Vinegar typically contains from 4% to 18% acetic acid by volume."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-vinegar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vinegar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with"